          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-74688",
          ":question/problem": "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-4 \\\\ 3x+2ky &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2k & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2k & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2k-6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-4 \\\\ (2k-6)y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=3,$ then the second equation is $0=10,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=3.$"
        },
