          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-74691",
          ":question/problem": "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y &=4 \\\\ -3x+ky &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & k & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & k & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & k-3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y &=4 \\\\ (k-3)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=3,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$,. So, we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $2x+2y=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=3.$"
